<Step id="STEP_0_INTAKE">
      <Goal>Read the Portal; detect scopes, graph, mode. (auto)</Goal>
      <Action>
        Scope names, types, and statuses come from the current portal's Scope Graph + Scopes table
        (`sdd-state`, per `AX_SCOPE_GRAPH_DETECTION`) already in context — the skill that dispatched
        this flow already called it; a fresh `sdd-state` call here is a wasted turn. Read
        `specs/README.md` itself ONLY for the one thing that snapshot does not carry: the
        dependency edges A→B of the Mermaid Scope Graph (absent portal → `H_NO_SPECS`). Before opening
        any scope spec, resolve the target set from the operator/session scaffold payload, then compute
        its transitive `depends-on` closure from that already-bounded portal graph. Read only target +
        closure scope specs and their linked module specs — specs only, never code
        (`AX_SPEC_IS_SOLE_SOURCE`). When the request intentionally means the whole approved project or
        names no narrower target, the explicit bounded fallback is every ✅ scope listed by the same
        `sdd-state` snapshot; say that fallback was selected. Never replace a missing/ambiguous target
        with filesystem discovery, glob/ls, or an all-spec archaeology pass.

        From this bounded set, enforce the decomposition gate fail-closed for each task-owning target:
        scope type must be
        present and unambiguous; product/library requires a non-empty Module Map whose explicit links
        resolve one-to-one to every canonical module spec on disk; interface cannot directly own tasks.
        Otherwise `H_SCOPE_NOT_DECOMPOSED`. Do not issue an extra discovery command for this check.
        Dependency-only interface scopes remain read context, never task owners. `infrastructure` is
        the sole valid flat scope. Every `sdd-new task` call repeats this guard
        defensively and must use the exact form
        <ToolLiteral role="future">npx gennady sdd-new task --scope <scope> --id <ACR-slug></ToolLiteral>;
        `--out` overrides only destination. If a lower-level caller omits `--scope`, the CLI accepts
        only one SCOPE_TYPE-bearing ancestor of that output path; zero/multiple owners fail with the
        exact request to pass `--scope`, and the same decomposition proof still runs.
        Detect mode per `AX_DIRECTIVE_MODE` / `AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT`
        — **detection table (directive-specific):**

        | Mode | Detection |
        |---|---|
        | `initial` | No `*.3-tasks.md` exists yet anywhere under the target scope (module / scope index absent) |
        | `extend-dag` | A `*.3-tasks.md` index already exists for the target scope and intake adds tickets to it |
        | — ambiguous | Index(es) exist but intake states neither add-tickets nor any mode verb → `H_AMBIGUOUS_MODE` |

        `extend-dag` reads existing tickets and preserves their IDs
        (`AX_EXTEND_DAG_PRESERVES_EXISTING_IDS`).
      </Action>
    </Step>

<Axiom id="AX_DIRECTIVE_MODE">
      Each directive runs in one of several modes, auto-detected from operator intake. Mode controls
      step branching and which axioms apply. The concrete mode set is directive-specific
      (e.g. `greenfield` / `refine` / `pivot`; `initial` / `extend-dag`; `initial` / `add-module` / `refine-module`).
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT">
      Mode detection is mechanical and table-driven from intake (artifact presence + operator verb).
      Ambiguous intake never defaults silently → HALT and ask the operator (binary disambiguation
      where possible). The detection table is directive-specific.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_SPEC_IS_SOLE_SOURCE">
      Phase reads specs only, never code; no project-wide search (grep / find / ripgrep)
      outside the reads explicitly listed by the directive. `audit` is the only directive
      permitted to read code; it alone resolves spec/code mismatch.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_SCOPE_GRAPH_DETECTION">
      Scope names, types, and statuses are read from the `sdd-state` snapshot already in context
      (its `[SCOPES]` section) — the calling skill already ran it; do not re-derive them by
      re-reading `specs/README.md`. Portal absent → no specs → HALT.

      `specs/README.md` itself is read for the one thing that snapshot does not carry: the
      dependency edges A→B (from the Mermaid `graph TD` Scope Graph, or the Scopes table
      `depends-on` column).

      Before reading scope specs, resolve the scaffold target set from the operator/session payload and
      compute its transitive `depends-on` closure from the portal graph. Read only that target+closure
      set. If the request intentionally targets the whole approved project or carries no narrower
      target, use every ✅ scope from the same snapshot as an explicit bounded fallback and say so;
      never use glob/ls/find or filesystem archaeology to broaden it.

      Cascade computation requires full scope graph traversal:
      - **Traversed-scopes** for a target scope = all scopes reachable via `depends-on` edges (transitive closure).
      - **Cascade** = union of Effective Rules from traversed-scopes + target-scope rules + (module rules if product/library) + task additions.

      | Scopes present | Result |
      |---|---|
      | Only infrastructure scopes | scaffold infra tasks only; no module trackers |
      | Mix of product/library + infra/contracts | full project; infra tasks = DAG roots |
      | No scopes at all | HALT: no spec input |
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_EXTEND_DAG_PRESERVES_EXISTING_IDS">
      **In `extend-dag`, existing Task-IDs and ticket files are immutable.**

      - New tasks get next sequential `TSK-NN` after max existing.
      - Existing tickets are NOT regenerated even if upstream specs changed — that is the orchestrator's reopen scenario via `sdd-execute`, not regeneration.
      - `add-scope` upstream → scaffold only for the new scope.
      - `add-module` or `refine-module` upstream → scaffold only for the new/changed surface; unchanged-surface tickets untouched.
      - Per-scope Cascade Table may update; existing tickets keep their old per-phase `Rules:` lists — orchestrator re-derives on reopen if the operator opts in.
      - DAG edges/IDs preserved; new tasks appended.
    </Axiom>